 			    sizeof(data), MEM_WRITE, CACHE_DATA)) {
```

`addr` is `uint32_t addr = *np;`. **Nothing else changed.**

## SETTLED PREMISES — re-inlined with their warrants

* **DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 and STM(1) p.A4-190 say to IGNORE THE LEAST SIGNIFICANT TWO BITS of the
  address.** The FAST path already gets that free from `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`.
* The SLOW path passed the **raw unmasked `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr &
  offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity (not word-rounded) and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy` — so an
  unaligned base spliced bytes across an architectural word boundary.
* **Symptom: the same instruction at the same unaligned base returns DIFFERENT BYTES according to
  host page-cache warmth**, a condition invisible to the guest architecture.
* **The mask must NOT move to function entry.** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running
  value and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked Rn.
* **In-tree precedent:** `generate_arm_multi.c` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` — the generated fast dispatch
  already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.
* **Measured (rung 3, unmodified `testarm`, cold debugger, pty-driven, `tlbdump` verifying which
  path each run took):** HEAD **17/25**, fix **25/25**, entry-mask mutant **20/25 (killed by the
  writeback rows)**, one-site-only mutants 21/25 each, null medium **6/25**, RESTORED **17/25 =
  HEAD**. The pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores the entry-mask mutant **22/22 — identical
  to a correct build.**

## QUESTIONS

1. **Is `addr & 0xfffffffc` the right mask, and is the LITERAL right for a `uint32_t`?** Would
   `~(uint32_t)3` or `& ~3` differ in any way that matters here (sign, promotion, width)? **Which
   does this file's own idiom prefer** — it uses `addr & 0xfff` and `addr &= 0xffc` elsewhere.
2. **Are these the ONLY two sites on the block-data-transfer path?** I grepped after editing and
   found 7 other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file, all of which I believe are the
   single-register load/store paths or the instruction fetch — **a different generator, out of
   scope.** Verify that. **A fix that repairs two of three sites of one idiom is this project's
   `#510` defect and has cost a round before.**
3. *** THUMB. *** The investigation recorded that Thumb PUSH/POP reach these same helpers from
   `cpu_arm.c`, and that **Thumb has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.** Does the fix cover the Thumb
   callers automatically by virtue of being in the shared helper, or is there a separate Thumb path
   that still passes a raw address? **Be exact — this is the question I am least confident about.**
4. **Does masking the address change anything for an ALIGNED base?** `addr & 0xfffffffc == addr`
   when already aligned, so I expect a strict no-op — **confirm, and say whether any caller can
   reach these lines with an address whose low bits are meaningful** (a device whose decode uses
   them, say).
5. **What does `memory_rw` do differently now?** With a word-aligned `paddr`, does `offset = paddr &
   offset_mask` plus the `memcpy` still behave for a 4-byte transfer that would previously have
   straddled a page boundary? **Is there a case where the OLD code straddled and the NEW code does
   not, changing which page faults?** That would be a behaviour change beyond the fix.
6. **THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT for this fix**, named explicitly: the guard kept, its effect nulled. My
   candidate is the entry-mask form, which is measured at 20/25. **Is there a SMALLER or QUIETER
   one** — e.g. masking only one of the two sites, or `& 0xfffffffe` (half-masking) — and would the
   shipped 25-row detector catch it? **Name the row that would red.**
7. **Is the comment honest?** It claims the entry-mask mutant scores 20/25 here and 22/22 on
   `arm_writeback_probe.py`. **A wrong number in a product comment gets reused.** Flag anything you
   cannot verify from the permitted files, and say plainly that you could not verify the scores (the
