2216-		/*  #354: the real `ldmia r1!,{r3,r4,ip,lr}` leaves those four
2217-		    registers holding the LAST 16 bytes loaded -- the final
2218-		    iteration's SECOND ldmia (src+16..+31). The fold moved the
2219-		    bytes with memcpy but never wrote the registers, so the guest
2220-		    read them stale ("publishes only r0/r1"). Direct host read,
2221-		    NO byteswap, base masked to a word -- matching the handler
2222-		    this fold emulates (multi_0x08b15018, generated by
2223:		    generate_arm_multi.c:117: `addr &= 0xffc`, then a plain
2224-		    `r[k] = p[k]`) and the page-cross / NULL-page bail-outs above,
2225-		    which delegate to that same handler. So fold == handler ==
2226-		    bail-out: an on-page copy cannot leave different registers
2227-		    than an identical copy that straddles a page. Each iteration
2228-		    overwrites r3/r4/ip/lr, so the final iteration's block
2229-		    persists, as the architecture leaves it.
2230-
2231-		    The mask is NOT cosmetic. ARM's LDM ignores addr[1:0] rather
2232-		    than requiring alignment, and this matcher matches code SHAPE,
2233-		    not register values, so a guest with r1 & 3 != 0 does fold:
2234-		    measured at r1 = SRC+1, the masked read publishes what the
2235-		    genuine sequence publishes, while `& 0xfff` would publish
