24187:A7.1.49 POP
24188-
24189-       15 14 13 12 11 10 9 8 7                                                                 0
24190-
24191-       1 0 1 1 1 1 0R           register_list
24192-
24193:       POP (Pop Multiple Registers) loads a subset (or possibly all) of the general-purpose registers R0-R7 and the
24194-       PC from the stack.
24195-
24196-       The general-purpose registers loaded can include the PC. If they do, the word loaded for the PC is treated
24197-       as an address and a branch occurs to that address. In ARMv5 and above, bit[0] of the loaded value
24198-       determines whether execution continues after this branch in ARM state or in Thumb state, as though the
--
24205-
24206-             MOV PC,(loaded_value)
24207-
24208-       Syntax           Is the list of registers, separated by commas and surrounded by { and }. The list is
24209-                        encoded in the register_list field of the instruction, by setting bit[i] to 1 if register Ri
24210:       POP <registers>  is included in the list and to 0 otherwise, for each of i=0 to 7. The R bit (bit[8]) is
24211-                        set to 1 if the PC is in the list and to 0 otherwise.
24212-       where:
24213-                        At least one register must be loaded. If bits[8:0] are all zero, the result is
24214-       <registers>      UNPREDICTABLE.
24215-
--
24263-assert end_address = address
24264-SP = end_address
24265-
24266-Usage
24267-
24268:Use POP for stack operations. A POP instruction with the PC in the register list can be used for an efficient
24269-procedure exit, as it restores saved registers, loads the PC with the return address, and updates the stack
24270-pointer with a single instruction.
