19674: A5.4  Addressing Mode 4 - Load and Store Multiple
19675: 
19676:       Load Multiple instructions load a subset (possibly all) of the general-purpose registers from memory. Store
19677:       Multiple instructions store a subset (possibly all) of the general-purpose registers to memory.
19678:       Load and Store Multiple addressing modes produce a sequential range of addresses. The lowest-numbered
19679:       register is stored at the lowest memory address and the highest-numbered register at the highest memory
19680:       address.
19681:       The general instruction syntax is:
19682: 
19683:       LDM|STM{<cond>}<addressing_mode> <Rn>{!}, <registers>{^}
19684: 
19685:       where <addressing_mode> is one of the following four addressing modes:
19686: 
19687:       1. IA (Increment After)
19688:               See Load and Store Multiple - Increment after on page A5-43.
19689: 
19690:       2. IB (Increment Before)
19691:               See Load and Store Multiple - Increment before on page A5-44.
19692: 
19693:       3. DA (Decrement After)
19694:               See Load and Store Multiple - Decrement after on page A5-45.
19695: 
19696:       4. DB (Decrement Before)
19697:               See Load and Store Multiple - Decrement before on page A5-46.
19698: 
19699:       There are also alternative mnemonics for these addressing modes, useful when LDM and STM are being used
19700:       to access a stack, see Load and Store Multiple addressing modes (alternative names) on page A5-47.
19701: 
