3241:           Other reasons for unaligned accesses to be UNPREDICTABLE
3242: 
3243:           The following exceptions to the behavior described in Table A2-10 on page A2-40 apply, causing the
3244:           resultant unaligned accesses to be UNPREDICTABLE:
3245: 
3246:           � An LDR instruction that loads the PC, has Addr[1:0] != 0b00, and is specified in the table as having
3247:                   Normal behavior instead has UNPREDICTABLE behavior.
3248: 
3249:                           Note
3250:                   The reason this applies only to LDR is that most other load instructions are UNPREDICTABLE regardless
3251:                   of alignment if the PC is specified as their destination register. The exceptions are LDM, RFE and Thumb
3252:                   POP. If Addr[1:0] != 0b00 for these instructions, the effective address of the transfer has its two least
3253:                   significant bits forced to 0 if A == 0 and U ==0, and otherwise the behavior specified in the table is
3254:                   either UNPREDICTABLE or Alignment Fault regardless of the destination register.
3255: 
3256:           � Any WLoad, WStore, WSync, Two-word or Multi-word instruction that accesses memory with the
3257:                   Strongly Ordered or Device memory attribute, has Addr[1:0] != 0b00, and is specified in the table
3258:                   as having Normal behavior instead has UNPREDICTABLE behavior.
3259: 
3260:           � Any Halfword instruction that accesses memory with the Strongly Ordered or Device memory
3261:                   attribute, has Addr[0] != 0, and is specified in the table as having Normal behavior instead has
3262:                   UNPREDICTABLE behavior.
3263: 
3264:           If any of these reasons applies, it overrides the behavior specified in the table.
3265: 
3266:                    Note
3267:           These reasons never cause Alignment Fault behavior to be overridden.
3268: 
3269:           ARM implementations are not required to ensure that the low-order address bits that make an access
3270:           unaligned are cleared from the address they send to memory. They can instead send the address as calculated
3271:           by the load/store instruction unchanged to memory, and require the memory system to ignore address[0] for
3272:           a halfword access and address[1:0] for a word access.
